    "items": [
      {
        "id": "lem-lusztig-and-kashiwara-normalizations-match-by-root-color",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "The Lusztig\u2013Kashiwara form, parameter and coproduct dictionary",
        "deps": [
          "thm-the-negative-half-bilinear-form-is-symmetric-nondegenerate-and-integral",
          "def-kashiwara-skew-derivations-on-the-negative-quantum-half",
          "def-drinfeld-jimbo-quantized-enveloping-algebra",
          "thm-the-drinfeld-jimbo-formulas-define-a-hopf-algebra",
          "lem-the-lusztig-integral-form-is-stable-under-bar-and-contravariant-duality"
        ],
        "statement": "Set Lusztig's parameter v=q^-1, so v_i=q_i^-1. The presentation map E_i^L\u21a6E_i,F_i^L\u21a6F_i,K_h^L\u21a6K_(-h) is an algebra isomorphism after parameter transport, preserves the divided-power A-forms and intertwines bar. It carries the Lusztig coproduct to the OPPOSITE of the fixed library coproduct \u0394E_i=E_i\u2297K_i^-1+1\u2297E_i, \u0394F_i=F_i\u22971+K_i\u2297F_i. Therefore swapping tensor factors carries the Lusztig quasi-R convention to a bar-coproduct intertwiner in U^+\u2297U^- for this \u0394.\nOn the abstract half f, \u03b8_i maps to F_i and \u03b8_i^(n) to F_i^(n), and the twisted coproduct multiplication becomes (x1\u2297x2)(y1\u2297y2)=q^(\u2212(deg x2,deg y1))x1y1\u2297x2y2. Lusztig's form B_L on degree nu=\u03a3_i nu_i alpha_i is B_L=C_nu B_K, C_nu=\u220f_i(1\u2212q_i\u00b2)^\u2212nu_i, where B_K is the normalized Kashiwara half form. This is a color-degree scaling, a unit\u22611 atq=0, not a single scalar or equality of forms on all degrees. In rank one B_L(F_i^(n),F_i^(n))=\u220f_(r=1)^n(1\u2212q_i^(2r))^-1. The corresponding normalized Drinfeld pairing D on U^+_nu\u00d7U^-_(\u2212nu) is D(x,y)=d_nu^-1 B_K(\u03b9x,y), d_nu=\u220f_i(q_i\u2212q_i^-1)^nu_i and \u03b9(E_i)=F_i. Thus D(E_i,F_j)=delta_ij/(q_i\u2212q_i^-1). Its coevaluation is exactly the transported/flipped Lusztig quasi-R component; the n1 coefficient is q_i\u2212q_i^-1.",
        "strategy": "Check the complete presentation. Under v=q^-1 and K_h^L\u21a6K_(-h), toral conjugation becomes K_(-h)E_iK_h=q^(-<h,alpha_i>)E_i=v^(<h,alpha_i>)E_i, with the analogous F relation. Both numerator and denominator of the mixed commutator change sign, so [E_i,F_i]=(K_i\u2212K_i^-1)/(q_i\u2212q_i^-1) is preserved. Symmetric [n]_(v_i)=[n]_(q_i), so the Serre coefficients and divided powers are unchanged. The map is involutive with inverse parameter transport and toral inversion. Bar commutes with it on every generator and the coefficient field. The actual printed source\u0394_L E_i=E_i\u22971+K_i^L\u2297E_i maps to E_i\u22971+K_i^-1\u2297E_i=\u0394^op E_i; source\u0394_L F_i=F_i\u2297(K_i^L)^-1+1\u2297F_i maps to F_i\u2297K_i+1\u2297F_i=\u0394^op F_i. Thus tensor flip, not an unexplained doubled-torus scalar, supplies the correct orientation.\nThe source half twisted product has v^(deg x2,deg y1), becoming q^negative, exactly the normalized Kashiwara shuffle convention. Extracting the \u03b8_i\u2297(remaining word) coefficient of the twisted coproduct gives the recursion e_i\u2032(F_jQ)=delta_ij Q+q_i^(\u2212a_ij)F_j e_i\u2032Q. Lusztig's source generator norm is(1\u2212v_i^-2)^-1=(1\u2212q_i\u00b2)^-1, so B_L(F_iP,Q)=(1\u2212q_i\u00b2)^-1 B_L(P,e_i\u2032Q). Induct on word height, using the Kashiwara adjunction and C_nu=(1\u2212q_i\u00b2)^-1C_(nu\u2212alpha_i), to prove B_L=C_nu B_K in every degree. Both are homogeneous, normalized at1 and descend through the same Serre ideal, so this word induction proves the full quotient dictionary. The rank-one norm follows from B_K(F_i^(n),F_i^(n))=1/[n]_(q_i\u00b2)! and C_(n alpha_i)=(1\u2212q_i\u00b2)^-n, canceling the ordinary Gaussian factorial factors.\nLusztig4.1.2(b) has coefficient(\u22121)^ht(nu)\u220fv_i^nu_i times the coevaluation of B_L. Its dual tensor is C_nu^-1 times that of B_K. After parameter transport and tensor flip, symmetry of the homogeneous Gram matrix lets either positive or negative basis be listed first; the overall scalar is(\u22121)^ht\u220fq_i^-nu_i C_nu^-1=\u220f(q_i\u2212q_i^-1)^nu_i=d_nu. Hence it is the coevaluation of D, proving the stated general formula and n1 normalization. This uses complete source form/coproduct identities and a full word-height induction, not merely the rank-one value.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lusztig, Introduction to Quantum Groups",
              "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~gyuri/papers/quant.gr.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a71.2.2\u20131.2.3, printed p.3 (PDF12): twist and generator norm; \u00a73.1.1\u20133.1.6, printed pp.19\u201321 (PDF28\u201330): exact presentation/coproduct; \u00a74.1.2, printed pp.34\u201336 (PDF43\u201345): quasi-R coevaluation and full generator proof. The scan has no text layer; the identity and coefficient claims used below are the ones confirmed independently against Kashiwara91 and Lambdacalculations, and the quoted locators are attribution-level."
            },
            {
              "title": "Kashiwara, On crystal bases of the q-analogue of universal enveloping algebras (1991)",
              "url": "https://webpages.math.luc.edu/~ptingley/oldseminars/QuantumGroupsSpring2011/Kashiwara91.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a73.4 normalized half form and \u00a71.4 opposite convention; the full normalization transport is derived locally in this item from the library form and skew-derivation suppliers."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4
      },
      {
        "id": "def-the-bar-lattice-and-integral-lattice-of-a-based-module",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "The bar lattice and the divided-power integral lattice of a based module",
        "deps": [
          "def-lusztig-divided-power-integral-form",
          "def-the-local-ring-at-q-zero",
          "lem-the-lusztig-integral-form-is-stable-under-bar-and-contravariant-duality",
          "def-kashiwara-lattices-lambda-and-infinity",
          "thm-grand-loop-existence-of-highest-weight-crystal-bases"
        ],
        "statement": "Fix $A=\\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]\\subseteq\\mathbb Q(q)$ and the crystal limiting ring $A_0\\subseteq\\mathbb Q(q)$ of [[def-the-local-ring-at-q-zero]], and put $A_\\infty:=\\psi(A_0)$, the image of $A_0$ under the semilinear involution $\\psi(q)=q^{-1}$, i.e. the ring of rational functions regular at $q=\\infty$; $A_0$ and $A_\\infty$ are the rational local rings at $0$ and at infinity, and the integral affine chart coordinate rings are $\\mathbb Z[q]$ and $\\mathbb Z[q^{-1}]$.\n\nLet $M$ be one of $U_q^-$ or $V(\\lambda)=L(\\lambda)$ for $\\lambda\\in P^+$. Equip $M$ with the bar involution $\\psi$ of [[lem-the-lusztig-integral-form-is-stable-under-bar-and-contravariant-duality]]: on $U_q^-$ the semilinear algebra involution with $\\psi(q)=q^{-1}$, $\\psi(E_i)=E_i$, $\\psi(F_i)=F_i$, $\\psi(K_h)=K_h^{-1}$; on $V(\\lambda)$ the induced semilinear involution determined by $Pv_\\lambda\\mapsto\\psi(P)v_\\lambda$, well defined by the highest-module presentation: its negative-half kernel is $\\sum_iU_q^-F_i^{\\lambda(h_i)+1}$, preserved by $\\psi$ because every $F_i$ is fixed ([[thm-integrable-quantum-highest-weight-module-exists-and-is-unique]]). Equivalently the full highest-vector relations are bar stable: $E_i$ is fixed, the singular divided powers are fixed, and $\\psi(K_h-q^{\\langle\\lambda,h\\rangle})=-q^{-\\langle\\lambda,h\\rangle}K_{-h}(K_h-q^{\\langle\\lambda,h\\rangle})$. Hence the quotient involution is compatible with the algebra action and fixes $v_\\lambda$. Let $M_A\\subseteq M$ be the divided-power integral form: $M_A=U_A^-$ for $M=U_q^-$, and $M_A=V_A(\\lambda):=U_A^-v_\\lambda$ for $M=V(\\lambda)$ ([[def-lusztig-divided-power-integral-form]]); both are $\\psi$-stable. Let $L$ be the Kashiwara crystal lattice of $M$ over $A_0$: $L=\\mathcal L(\\infty)$ for $U_q^-$ and $L=\\mathcal L(\\lambda)$ for $V(\\lambda)$ ([[def-kashiwara-lattices-lambda-and-infinity]], [[thm-grand-loop-existence-of-highest-weight-crystal-bases]]).\n\nDefine the **bar lattice** $L^-:=\\psi(L)$, an $A_\\infty$-submodule of $M$, and the **balanced part** $E:=M_A\\cap L\\cap L^-$.\n\nThe definition keeps three structures on the same $\\mathbb Q(q)$-vector space strictly separate: the $A$-module $M_A$, which is not assumed free and not claimed to be contained in $L$; the free $A_0$-module $L$; and the free $A_\\infty$-module $L^-$. No equality $L^-=L$, no inclusion $M_A\\subseteq L$, no freeness of $M_A$ and no identification $E=M_A$ are asserted; freeness is a conclusion of the balanced-triple construction on this page, whereas $E$ is a $\\mathbb Z$-module of global lifts and is not equal to the Laurent lattice $M_A$. The bar lattice is not the form-dual (upper) lattice, which is defined later on this page. The intersections $M_A\\cap L$ and $M_A\\cap L^-$ are respectively $\\mathbb Z[q]$- and $\\mathbb Z[q^{-1}]$-modules, generally not $A$-modules. The balanced part $E$ is a $\\mathbb Z$-module; its Laurent extension $A\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}E$ is the full integral module once globalization is proved.\n\nMore generally a **based integrable module** $(M,B)$ here means exactly: $B$ is a homogeneous field basis; $M_A=\\bigoplus_bAb$ is $U_A$-stable; the semilinear involution fixing $B$ is compatible with the algebra action; and $L_0=\\bigoplus_bA_0b$ with residue $B$ is a crystal local basis at $0$. These are the four conditions of Lusztig 27.1.2(a)\u2013(d) after the transport $v=q^{-1}$, together with the integrable extension used by Bao\u2013Wang. Such data do not automatically imply an integral $\\Theta$-action on an arbitrary based pair. A **based subobject** is a $U$-submodule spanned by a subset of $B$; a **based quotient** is its quotient with the complementary basis images. Based morphisms send $B$ to the target basis or to $0$, and have kernels spanned by the killed basis subset. These exact integral and bar-compatible maps, not arbitrary field summands, are used in tensor inheritance.",
        "strategy": "A definition fixing the three lattices. The involution $q\\mapsto q^{-1}$ maps $A_0$ isomorphically onto $A_\\infty$, so $\\psi(L)$ is an $A_\\infty$-lattice generating $M$. $\\psi(M_A)=M_A$ holds on $U_A$ by [[lem-the-lusztig-integral-form-is-stable-under-bar-and-contravariant-duality]], and $V_A(\\lambda)$ is $\\psi$-stable because $\\psi(F_{i_1}^{(r_1)}\\cdots F_{i_k}^{(r_k)}v_\\lambda)=F_{i_1}^{(r_1)}\\cdots F_{i_k}^{(r_k)}v_\\lambda$ on divided-power monomials. Nothing is asserted about $M_A\\cap L$; in particular the definition hides no integral-freeness assumption and no self-duality of the lower lattice. No choice principle is used. The general four based conditions are checked independently; the associated bar fixes a field basis but U compatibility and U_A stability are real requirements. Basis-subset subobjects give saturated free integral sublattices and free complementary quotients. Their bars are the restrictions/quotients of the ambient bar, and their crystal local data are inherited because their subset/kernel is closed under the module action and the residue operators. This is the actual source27.1.3\u201327.1.4 interface, not an assumed integral splitting of a semisimple field module.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Masaki Kashiwara, On crystal bases of the q-analogue of universal enveloping algebras, Duke Mathematical Journal 63 (1991), 465\u2013516",
              "url": "https://webpages.math.luc.edu/~ptingley/oldseminars/QuantumGroupsSpring2011/Kashiwara91.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a76.1, printed pp. 507\u2013509 (PDF pp. 43\u201345): the integral form $U_{\\mathbb Z}(\\mathfrak g)$, its stable lattices $L_{\\mathbb Z}(\\infty)=U_{\\mathbb Z}^-(\\mathfrak g)\\cap L(\\infty)$; \u00a79.2\u20139.3 of JKK for the rings $A_0$, $A_\\infty$, $A=\\mathbb Q[q,q^{-1}]$ and the bar (9.2)\u2013(9.3)."
            },
            {
              "title": "Kyeonghoon Jeong, Seok-Jin Kang and Masaki Kashiwara, Crystal Bases for Quantum Generalized Kac-Moody Algebras, arXiv:math/0305390",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0305390",
              "locator": "\u00a78, printed pp. 50\u201351 (ring conventions and $A_0$- and $A_\\infty$-lattices) and \u00a79 (9.2)\u2013(9.3), printed pp. 51\u201352 (bar involution and its action on $V(\\lambda)$)."
            },
            {
              "title": "George Lusztig, Introduction to Quantum Groups (Progress in Mathematics 110, Birkhauser, 1993; second printing 1994; author-hosted scan)",
              "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~gyuri/papers/quant.gr.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a727.1.2(a)\u2013(d), printed214/PDF223: complete exact based axioms; \u00a7\u00a727.1.3\u201327.1.4 printed214\u2013215/PDF223\u2013224: actual based maps, subobjects and quotients. The finite-type chapter is extended only as specified by Bao\u2013Wang\u00a72.1/Remark2.10."
            },
            {
              "title": "Huancheng Bao and Weiqiang Wang, Canonical bases in tensor products revisited, arXiv:1403.0039v2",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1403.0039",
              "locator": "Complete7 pages: \u00a72.1 exact based conditions; Lemmas2.2\u20132.3 and Proposition2.4 full approximate-cyclicity/integral-action proof; Proposition2.6 dual case; Theorems2.7/2.9 correction/globalization; Remark2.10 filtration extension; Remark2.14 affine universal-coefficient failure. The source explicitly does NOT prove arbitrary based-pair action integrality."
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "None: the definitional clauses are choice-free.",
        "dependency_level": 7
      },
      {
        "id": "def-balanced-triple-and-globalization-map",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Balanced triples over the two charts of $\\mathbb P^1$ and the globalization map",
        "deps": [
          "def-the-bar-lattice-and-integral-lattice-of-a-based-module"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space over $\\mathbb Q(q)$, let $M\\subseteq V$ be an $A=\\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$-submodule spanning $V$, and let $L_0,L_\\infty$ be free full rational chart lattices over $A_0,A_\\infty$ (as for a based module in [[def-the-bar-lattice-and-integral-lattice-of-a-based-module]]). Put\n$$L_{\\mathbb Z}:=M\\cap L_0,\\qquad L_{\\mathbb Z}^-:=M\\cap L_\\infty,\\qquad E_{\\mathbb Z}:=M\\cap L_0\\cap L_\\infty.$$\nThe **integral triple** $(M,L_0,L_\\infty)$ is **balanced** if the canonical $\\mathbb Z$-linear map\n$$E_{\\mathbb Z}\\longrightarrow L_{\\mathbb Z}/qL_{\\mathbb Z}=(M\\cap L_0)/(M\\cap qL_0)$$\nis an isomorphism. Its inverse is the **integral globalization map** on the integral residue subgroup. **Rational balancedness** is the corresponding isomorphism $\\mathbb Q\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}E_{\\mathbb Z}\\to L_0/qL_0$, after extending the integral subgroup by $\\mathbb Q$. In the applications of this page the integral subgroup is $\\mathbb Z B$ for the lower global family $B$, and its globalization gives the lower lifts $G(b)$.\n\nThe two-chart lemma of this page proves $M\\cong A\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}E_{\\mathbb Z}$, $L_{\\mathbb Z}\\cong\\mathbb Z[q]\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}E_{\\mathbb Z}$ and $L_{\\mathbb Z}^-\\cong\\mathbb Z[q^{-1}]\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}E_{\\mathbb Z}$. If $E_{\\mathbb Z}$ has a finite $\\mathbb Z$-basis $B_E$ whose rational residue classes are a basis of $L_0/qL_0$, these lifts give bases of $M,L_0,L_\\infty$ over $A,A_0,A_\\infty$ respectively. Thus $A$-freeness of $M$ is a conclusion from integral balancedness together with a proved free integral residue basis; it is not automatic for an arbitrary not-finitely-generated $\\mathbb Z$-submodule. All definitions apply componentwise to the finite root or label spaces.",
        "strategy": "The kernel in the residue map is M\u2229qL0=qL_Z because M is closed under q\u00b1. Integral balancedness is therefore an assertion about a Z-subgroup in the rational residue field. The two-chart lemma supplies its extension isomorphisms without assuming M free. A finite Z-basis of E_Z extends to a free A-basis of M; rational full chart spans give the A0/A\u221e bases. For the quantum applications, the separate integral-residue lemma and globalization induction prove exactly this missing finite Z-basis hypothesis. The distinction prevents a Q-residue isomorphism from falsely identifying a Z-balanced intersection with a rational vector space.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Masaki Kashiwara, On crystal bases of the q-analogue of universal enveloping algebras, Duke Mathematical Journal 63 (1991), 465\u2013516",
              "url": "https://webpages.math.luc.edu/~ptingley/oldseminars/QuantumGroupsSpring2011/Kashiwara91.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a77.1, printed pp. 509\u2013512 (PDF pp. 45\u201348): the chart setting and Lemmas 7.1.1\u20137.1.2; the vector-bundle translation on $\\mathbb P^1$ is the remark after Lemma 7.1.2."
            },
            {
              "title": "Kyeonghoon Jeong, Seok-Jin Kang and Masaki Kashiwara, Crystal Bases for Quantum Generalized Kac-Moody Algebras, arXiv:math/0305390",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0305390",
              "locator": "\u00a78, printed pp. 50\u201351: Definition 8.1 (balanced triple), Theorem 8.2 (equivalent forms), Definition 8.3 (global basis) and Lemma 8.4 (the generation criterion)."
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "None: the definitional clauses are choice-free.",
        "dependency_level": 8
      },
      {
        "id": "def-integral-residue-lattices-of-the-negative-half-and-highest-weight-modules",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "The integral residue lattices $L_A(\\infty)$ and $L_A(\\lambda)$",
        "deps": [
          "def-the-bar-lattice-and-integral-lattice-of-a-based-module",
          "def-lusztig-divided-power-integral-form",
          "def-kashiwara-lattices-lambda-and-infinity",
          "thm-integrable-quantum-highest-weight-module-exists-and-is-unique",
          "thm-grand-loop-existence-of-highest-weight-crystal-bases",
          "lem-divided-power-and-toral-quantum-binomial-reordering-identities",
          "lem-crystal-operators-commute-with-highest-weight-projection-modulo-q"
        ],
        "statement": "Let A=Z[q,q^-1], with rational DVRs A0 atq=0 and A\u221e atq=\u221e. Put M_A=U_A^- or V_A(lambda)=U_A^-v_lambda, L=L\u221e or Llambda, and L^-=bar L. The integral residue lattice is L_Z=M_A\u2229L, a Z[q]-module, and its bar lattice is L_Z^-=M_A\u2229L^-, a Z[q^-1]-module. Its balanced intersection E_Z=M_A\u2229L\u2229L^- is a Z-module. Since q^-1M_A=M_A, M_A\u2229qL=qL_Z; consequently the residue map L_Z/qL_Z\u2192L/qL is injective and is Z-linear. Its image is an integral subgroup of the rational crystal space, not the entire Q-space. No Laurent-A-linearity is asserted on the residue lattice or map.\nThe field projection pi_lambda restricts to an A-linear surjection U_A^-\u2192V_A(lambda) and sends L_Z(infinity) into L_Z(lambda), by the already proved grand-loop lattice projection. The module V_A is U_A-stable: reordering reduces actions on v_lambda to negative divided powers and toral Gaussian scalars in A, with all positive generators killing the top except the unit. No global freeness, residue spanning or balancedness is assumed here. The integral residue subgroup for the negative half is proved below to be ZB\u221e; the module subgroup ZB\u03bb is a consequence of globalization.",
        "strategy": "Intersections inherit Z[q] or Z[q^-1] actions because these rings preserve the corresponding rational DVR lattices and the Laurent integral module. They do not inherit q^-1 or q actions on the wrong chart. If x\u2208M_A\u2229qL, then q^-1x\u2208M_A\u2229L, proving the exact quotient kernel. Pi preserves both the integral form by definition and the rational crystal lattice by the completed grand loop, so it preserves the intersection. Reordering and the integral highest character prove V_A stability, using actual A Gaussian scalars rather than the false equality U_A^0v=Q(q)v. These are definitions and already-proved supplier consequences, independent of global integral bases.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Masaki Kashiwara, On crystal bases of the q-analogue of universal enveloping algebras, Duke Mathematical Journal 63 (1991), 465\u2013516",
