        ],
        "statement": "Let $i\\in I$ and let $e_i'$ be the skew derivation of [[def-kashiwara-skew-derivations-on-the-negative-quantum-half]], with string decomposition $u=\\sum_lF_i^{(l)}u_l$, $u_l\\in\\ker e_i'$ ([[thm-the-i-string-decomposition-of-the-negative-half]]).\n\n(i) If $u\\in U_A^-$ then all $u_l$ lie in $U_A^-$. The integral projector is\n$$P_i=\\sum_l(-1)^lq_i^{-l(l-1)/2}F_i^{(l)}(e_i')^l,\\qquad u_l=q_i^{l(l-1)/2}P_i(e_i')^lu,$$\nwhere all powers of $e_i'$ are ordinary powers, not divided by $[l]_i!$.\n\n(ii) Define\n$$I_{(i,n)}^A:=U_A^-\\cap F_i^nU_q^-=\\bigoplus_{l\\ge n}F_i^{(l)}\\bigl(\\ker e_i'\\cap U_A^-\\bigr)=\\sum_{l\\ge n}F_i^{(l)}U_A^-,$$\n$$J_{(i,n)}^A:=\\pi_\\lambda\\bigl(I_{(i,n)}^A\\bigr)=\\sum_{l\\ge n}F_i^{(l)}V_A(\\lambda),$$\nwith $\\pi_\\lambda(P)=Pv_\\lambda$ and $V_A(\\lambda)=U_A^-v_\\lambda$ as in [[def-integral-residue-lattices-of-the-negative-half-and-highest-weight-modules]]. The latter is an $A$-submodule inside the field string-image subspace $F_i^nV(\\lambda)$, not a $U_q$-submodule or a $U_A$-stable lattice in general; its equality with $V_A(\\lambda)\\cap F_i^nV(\\lambda)$ is proved after the adapted global basis construction, not presumed here.\n\n(iii) The integral residue of $I_{(i,n)}^A\\cap\\mathcal L(\\infty)$ is the free $\\mathbb Z$-span of $\\mathcal B(\\infty)\\cap\\tilde f_i^n\\mathcal B(\\infty)$, and the corresponding rational residue of $F_i^nU_q^-\\cap\\mathcal L(\\infty)$ is its $\\mathbb Q$-span. Integral balancedness of the zero/infinity triple of $I_{(i,n)}^A$ is a conclusion of the subsequent string-ideal induction, not an input.\n\n(iv) On every $U_A$-stable integrable module lattice $M_A$, if a label $\\mu$ satisfies $\\langle h_i,\\mu\\rangle=-n\\le-1$, then every $u\\in(M_A)_\\mu$ satisfies the finite identity\n$$u=\\sum_{k\\ge n}(-1)^{k-n}\\binom{k-1}{k-n}_iF_i^{(k)}E_i^{(k)}u,$$\nand consequently $(M_A)_\\mu=\\sum_{k\\ge n}F_i^{(k)}(M_A)_{\\mu+k\\alpha_i}$. This is the exact boundary generation supplier used in the string-ideal induction.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 **The integral string decomposition and projector.** For $u\\in U_A^-$ the unique decomposition of [F2] has $u_l=q_i^{l(l-1)/2}P_i(e_i')^lu$ with $P_i=\\sum_l(-1)^lq_i^{-l(l-1)/2}F_i^{(l)}(e_i')^l$, the powers of $e_i'$ being ordinary; by [F1] the coefficients $q_i^{\\pm l(l-1)/2}$ and the operators $F_i^{(l)}$ and $(e_i')^l$ preserve $U_A^-$, so every $u_l$ lies in $U_A^-$. [F1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 **The finite Gaussian identity.** Fix integers $m\\ge n\\ge1$, put $z=q_i$, $Q=z^2$, define $(Q;Q)_r:=\\prod_{j=1}^r(1-Q^j)$ for $r\\ge0$, and set $H(t)=\\prod_{j=1}^{n-1}(1-Q^{-j}t)\\prod_{j=1}^{m-n}(1-Q^jt)$, a polynomial of degree $m-1$. For every polynomial of degree $\\le m-1$ the finite $q$-binomial theorem of [F5] gives $\\sum_{k=0}^m(-1)^kQ^{k(k-1)/2-k(m-1)}\\binom{m}{k}_QH(Q^k)=0$: on the monomial $t^s$ the sum factors as $\\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}(1-Q^{j+s-m+1})$, which contains the zero factor $j=m-1-s$. For $1\\le k<n$ one has $H(Q^k)=0$; for $k\\ge n$, $H(Q^k)/[(Q;Q)_{n-1}(Q;Q)_{m-n}]=\\binom{k-1}{n-1}_Q\\binom{m-n+k}{k}_Q$; and the $k=0$ term equals $(-1)^{n-1}Q^{-n(n-1)/2}$. Multiplying the vanishing sum by $(-1)^nQ^{n(n-1)/2}$ divided by the two factorials turns it into $\\sum_{k=n}^m(-1)^{k-n}\\binom{k-1}{k-n}_z\\binom{m-n+k}{k}_z\\binom{m}{k}_z=1$, using $\\binom{a}{b}_z=z^{-b(a-b)}\\binom{a}{b}_Q$ and the exponent identity $k(k-1)/2-k(m-1)+n(n-1)/2=-\\frac{(n-1)(k-n)+k(m-n)+k(m-k)}2$. [F5, algebra]\n\n2.1 **String ideals.** By [F2], $u\\in F_i^nU_q^-$ if and only if $u_l=0$ for $l<n$; hence $I_{(i,n)}=U_A^-\\cap F_i^nU_q^-$ equals $\\bigoplus_{l\\ge n}F_i^{(l)}(\\ker e_i'\\cap U_A^-)$ directly, and this equals the sum $\\sum_{l\\ge n}F_i^{(l)}U_A^-$ because each displayed direct summand is contained in $F_i^{(l)}U_A^-$, while multiplying an integral string expansion by $F_i^{(l)}$ yields only positions $l+m\\ge l$ with integral coefficients $\\binom{l+m}{l}_i$; the sum is not direct as written. Applying the $A$-linear projection $\\pi_\\lambda$ of [[def-integral-residue-lattices-of-the-negative-half-and-highest-weight-modules]] gives $J_{(i,n)}^A=\\sum_{l\\ge n}F_i^{(l)}V_A(\\lambda)$, an $A$-submodule of the field subspace $F_i^nV(\\lambda)$, with no $U_q$- or $U_A$-stability asserted. [F2, F6, step 1.1]\n\n2.2 **Boundary generation on a module lattice.** By [[thm-unique-kashiwara-i-string-decomposition]], reduce to an admissible string vector $u=F_i^{(m)}v$ with $E_iv=0$ and top weight $2m-n$. Admissibility gives $m\\le2m-n$, hence $m\\ge n$; the formulas of [[thm-quantized-sl-two-string-formulas]] give $F_i^{(k)}E_i^{(k)}u=\\binom{m-n+k}{k}_z\\binom{m}{k}_zu$ for $k\\le m$ and $0$ for $k>m$. Substituting step 1.2, the alternating sum of step (iv) is $u$: the coefficients are exactly those of the proved identity, with finite support because $k\\le m$. Every $u\\in(M_A)_\\mu$ is a finite sum of such string vectors, and all operators and coefficients involved lie in $A$ and preserve $M_A$ ([[def-lusztig-divided-power-integral-form]]), so $(M_A)_\\mu=\\sum_{k\\ge n}F_i^{(k)}(M_A)_{\\mu+k\\alpha_i}$, with finiteness supplied by integrability. [F5, F6, step 1.2]\n\n3.1 **String-ideal residues.** By [F2], the field image of $\\tilde f_i^n$ is $F_i^nU_q^-$. On this image $\\tilde f_i^n\\tilde e_i^n$ is the identity. Both shifts preserve $U_A^-$ and $\\mathcal L(\\infty)$ by [F1], [F3], so $I_{(i,n)}^A\\cap\\mathcal L(\\infty)=\\tilde f_i^n L_{\\mathbb Z}$. Taking residues and using [F4] gives exactly the integral span of $\\tilde f_i^n\\mathcal B(\\infty)$; the same argument over $A_0$ gives its rational span. [F1, F2, F3, F4, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 **Conclusion.** Steps 1.1\u20132.1 prove (i) and (ii), step 3.1 proves (iii), step 2.2 proves (iv); integral balancedness of the string-ideal triple is left to the subsequent induction and no saturation of $J_{(i,n)}^A$, no $U_q$-structure on it and no divided-derivation integrality are asserted. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 2.2] \u220e",
