        "strategy": "Use the full printed Lusztig4.1.2 proof in the exact local normalization map. Its source \u0398_L components use the B_L coevaluation with scalar(\u22121)^height \u220fv_i^nu_i. The proved presentation map carries \u0394_L to \u0394^op and bar compatibly; tensor flip therefore carries its generator identity to \u0394u\u0398=\u0398bar\u0394u. The degree-color computation already proves its tensors are exactly the D-coevaluation. To reproduce the actual source proof locally, expand \u0398_nu in any homogeneous half bases with an unknown coefficient matrix. Commuting E_i/F_i through the opposite half uses the two skew derivations from the defining Serre presentation, a word-induction identity: the toral numerators contain left/right skew derivatives and denominator q_i\u2212q_i^-1. Pair the remaining tensor factor with each basis vector; the two coevaluation expansion identities turn the four resulting derivative equations into the defining coproduct adjunction equations of D. Thus the prescribed coefficient matrix solves all generator equations, including both toral numerator coefficients, not a guessed single projector equation. In the difference of two normalized solutions, take minimal positive height. The lower terms in the derivative equations are0, forcing every coefficient vector in a positive-degree half to have all skew derivatives0. The established separation supplier makes it0. Pairing with every basis vector then kills the entire coefficient matrix, proving uniqueness by height induction. Multiplicativity extends generator equations to all u. Directly extracting homogeneous degree gives the two displayed commutator recursions; their first degree fixes the coefficient q_i\u2212q_i^-1. Rank-one inversion of the stated D divided norm gives the displayed full Gaussian coefficient. This closes the field convention gap without importing an unproved dictionary from a doubled torus.",
