        "statement": "For every dominant $\\lambda$ and every symmetrizable datum, the integral highest lattice $V_A(\\lambda)=U_A^-v_\\lambda$ has its genuine lower global $A$-basis $G_\\lambda(\\mathcal B(\\lambda))$ of [[def-lower-global-basis-of-a-quantum-highest-weight-module]]. Hence for any unital base change $A\\to S$ of $A=\\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$, including nonflat specializations at a root of unity with $\\zeta$ invertible, the tensor fibre $V_A(\\lambda)\\otimes_AS$ has the corresponding $S$-basis $G_\\lambda(b)\\otimes1$, indexed by the same crystal elements, and for $S\\ne0$ has identical finite weight ranks.\n\nAt a characteristic-zero field specialization $q=1$, the positive toral quotient and the local $q=1$ comparison identify the fibre with the classical simple integrable highest weight module and its Weyl\u2013Kac character. The zero and infinity chart lattices are obtained separately from the $\\mathbb Z[q]$- and $\\mathbb Z[q^{-1}]$-spans of the global vectors followed by rational discrete-valuation-ring extension; there is no map $A\\to A_0$ sending the unit $q$ to $0$. At roots of unity the free integral fibre basis still survives, but no simplicity, semisimplicity or equality with a naive ordinary-generator specialization is asserted. For a specialization to a field, if $\\zeta_i^2$ has order $l>1$, then the Gaussian factorial $[r]_{q_i^2}!$ vanishes once $r\\ge l$; if $\\zeta_i^2=1$ in characteristic zero every factor equals $j$ and that vanishing argument does not apply. Thus the algebra or module root-of-unity boundary is distinguished from the unconditional free-basis base-change fact.",
        "strategy": "A direct sum of free A-basis vectors tensors to the corresponding free S-basis for every unital A-algebra S; flatness and injectivity are unnecessary. The q=1 module comparison is the proved local/global highest-module theorem and exact positive toral quotient, preserving every weight rank and the classical character. The integral balanced chart factorization uses Z[q]G or Z[q^-1]G before localizing at0 or infinity; q is a unit in A and cannot be specialized to0 there. At root-of-unity values use [r]_(\u03b6_i\u00b2)!=\u220f_(j=1)^r(1+\u03b6_i\u00b2+...+\u03b6_i^(2(j\u22121))). If \u03b6_i\u00b2 has order l>1 its l-th factor is0; if it is1 each factor isj. These exact scalar computations show why ordinary divided-generator identification can fail, while the genuine integral basis itself still base changes. No unsupported root-of-unity simplicity theorem is imported.",
