        "statement": "In symmetric finite ADE type (all d_i=1), transport Lusztig's parameter by v=q^-1, the abstract half generators \u03b8_i^(n)\u21a6F_i^(n), and full torus K_h^L\u21a6K_(-h). Divided-power integral forms and bar agree under this transport; the half forms agree by B_L=C_nu B_K, not by an unqualified identity. Under this exact dictionary Lusztig's canonical LOWER basis equals the Kashiwara lower global basis of U^-, and its canonical highest-module quotient bases equal the corresponding lower global bases for every dominant label lambda (using the explicit regular realization). The signed canonical set equals \u00b1G\u221e; the positive canonical choice is matched by its actual Ti,n top-string recursion. No equality of upper and lower bases, or unproved 'unitriangular word-basis' characterization, is asserted.",
        "strategy": "Use the proved full form/parameter/coproduction dictionary. The actual source14.2.3/14.2.4 defines the canonical signed set as integral bar-fixed elements x with B_L(x,x)\u22081+qZ[[q]] after v=q^-1. Every G\u221e is integral/bar fixed and lies in L\u221e; the Kashiwara residue pairing is orthonormal and its integral values lie in A_Z\u2282Z[[q]], while C_nu is a unit\u22611 with integer power series. Thus \u00b1G\u221e satisfies the signed norm condition. Conversely that norm condition and C_nu unit imply B_K(x,x)\u2208A0; the already-proved norm criterion puts x in L\u221e. Bar-fixedness puts it in bar L\u221e too. Integral globalization therefore writes x as a finite Z-combination of the lower G\u221e. Its norm residue is a positive sum of integer squares1, so x=\u00b1G\u221e. This proves the entire signed-basis characterization locally rather than importing its geometric existence proof.\nThe source14.3.2 defines Bi;n by the left string ideals and Ti,n by the UNIQUE signed element b\u2032 of exact string positionn such that F_i^(n)b\u2212b\u2032 is an integral linear combination of strictly higher string positions. The already-proved adapted global ideal bases identify this signed partition with the crystal epsilon partition. The explicit top-string transition from integral globalization gives Ti,n(G\u221e(b))=G\u221e(f\u0303_i^n b) when e\u0303_i b=0. The sign is+1, fixed by that exact coefficient1 quotient relation. Source14.4.2 defines the positive canonical basis recursively from1 by these Ti,n transitions. The positive Kashiwara crystal has precisely the same recursion: every positive-height b has some epsilon_i=n>0, with top e\u0303_i^n b in smaller height, and b=f\u0303_i^n(e\u0303_i^n b). Induction on height therefore identifies the positive source canonical basis with G\u221e, eliminating the signed ambiguity. This verifies the positive construction, not just their almost-orthonormal norms or names.\nSource14.4.11\u201314.4.12 defines the highest-module canonical basis by the based quotient of the half, with killed right-string-ideal elements omitted. The completed local based-quotient theorem gives exactly the same zero/survival formula on G\u221e. The full parameter/torus map preserves the highest character: K_h^L acts by v^<h,lambda>=q^(-<h,lambda>), the eigenvalue of K_(-h) on the library highest module. The early auxiliary regular datum discharges the source Y-regularity and transports the original label weights back. Hence these quotient bases coincide. This argument preserves the approved ADE conclusion without using upper-duality or identifying upper vectors with lower ones.",
