        "statement": "For every dominant label weight $\\lambda$, let $V_A=U_Av_\\lambda=U_A^-v_\\lambda$ be the integral highest lattice inside the simple integrable module $L(\\lambda)$, over $A=\\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$. Then each label-weight component of $V_A$ is finite free of rank $\\dim_{\\mathbb Q(q)}L(\\lambda)_\\mu$, not merely torsionfree, and $V_A$ spans the field module. For every characteristic-zero field $k$ specialized at $q=1$, the fibre has the same weight dimensions and is the classical simple integrable highest weight module of the explicitly regularized realization, transported back to the original label lattice; hence its character is the Weyl\u2013Kac character, the finite Weyl character in finite type. The integral quotient $U_A^-/\\sum_i\\sum_{s>\\lambda(h_i)}U_A^-F_i^{(s)}$ is $V_A(\\lambda)$; its field extension is $L(\\lambda)$ in every symmetrizable type.",
        "strategy": "The simultaneous global construction and its based-quotient theorem give a Z[q\u00b1]-basis of U_A^-v_lambda: each negative global basis element maps to the corresponding highest-module global element if its crystal image survives, and to0 otherwise (Kashiwara91 Theorem7(ii), Section7.5.2). Thus each finite weight lattice is actually free over A, with rank its field dimension. The early local-q1 deformation/universality theorem already proves the simple quantum module, field character and equality with the integrable Serre quotient using DVR-free lattices; it is not dependent on this global result. Base change of the newly proved integral weight basis preserves dimensions. Algebraic positive specialization and finite-support regularization identify the classical fibre and its character, and the classical Weyl-Kac theorem gives the explicit formula. Perfect-pairing/regularity/fundamental-weight hypotheses used by the sources are established by the actual auxiliary-datum lemma. This proves the stronger original global Z-lattice assertion without assuming it as a balanced-triple premise.",
