        ],
        "statement": "Bar-fixedness and a prescribed residue alone do not imply balancedness, global parametrization or uniqueness. First, in the two-dimensional sl2 module use L\u2032=A0(v+w)\u2295qA0v and its ACTUAL bar lattice A\u221e(v+w)\u2295q^-1A\u221ev. With V_A=Av\u2295Aw, its balanced part is Z(v+w), whose residue image misses the valid nonzero class qv+qL\u2032. The vector v+w is bar fixed but L\u2032 fails the crystal-lattice weight/stability axioms; it is a general-lattice failure of existence/parametrization, not by itself a uniqueness counterexample.\nFor a genuine separate nonuniqueness witness use the one-dimensional trivial module Q(q)v with integral lattice Av, bar v=v, and crystal lattice L\u2033=q^-1A0v (all operators0) with bar L\u2033=qA\u221ev. Its balanced intersection is Zq^-1v\u2295Zv\u2295Zqv, mapping onto the Z-residue generator q^-1v+qL\u2033 with nonzero kernel. The two DISTINCT bar-fixed integral vectors (q+q^-1)v and(q+q^-1)v+v have the same nonzero crystal residue. Thus even a valid scaled crystal lattice plus bar invariance/congruence does not force unique global lifts without the actual balanced map. Neither example is the canonical top-normalized Kashiwara triple of a highest module.",
        "strategy": "For L\u2032 use a=v+w,t=v. Its integral zero intersection is Z[q]a\u2295qZ[q]t; its infinity intersection is Z[q^-1]a\u2295q^-1Z[q^-1]t, because bar sends qv toq^-1v. The triple intersection is Za, while the integral zero residue is Za\u2295Zqv. This proves the missing qv class; v itself is not in L\u2032, so v+qL\u2032 is undefined and never asserted. The elementary e\u0303a=v and coefficient calculation show the lattice is free/full but not crystal stable or weight decomposed, distinguishing this failure from the next example.\nIn the trivial module all Kashiwara operators vanish; every homogeneous full free scaled A0 lattice is a genuine crystal lattice, and q^-1v is its residue basis representative. The simultaneous Laurent coefficient bounds for L\u2033 and bar L\u2033 are exponents\u22121\u2264k\u22641, giving exactly the displayed rank3 Z intersection. Reducing modulo qL\u2033=A0v kills v and qv but not q^-1v. Both proposed integral vectors are fixed by q\u21a6q^-1, lie in the simultaneous intersection and have coefficient1 on q^-1v, hence the same nonzero crystal class; their difference v\u22600 is in the kernel. This proves the promised nonuniqueness directly. The canonical globalization theorem requires its exact integral balanced map, not merely a crystal lattice, and remains unchanged.",
