        ],
        "statement": "For rank-one V(m omega), use w_n=F^(n)v_m, 0\u2264n\u2264m, the lower global basis and lower integral lattice M_A=\u2295A w_n. Its normalized polarization has B(w_n,w_s)=delta_ns d_(m,n), with d_(m,n)=q^(n(m\u2212n))[m choose n]_q. Thus its upper form-dual global basis is w_n^up=d_(m,n)^\u22121w_n and M_A^vee=\u2295A d_(m,n)^\u22121 w_n. These two explicit A-lattices are the object being compared; a numeric finite index is not presumed. For m=1, d=1 at both positions and the bases coincide. For m=2, d_(2,0)=d_(2,2)=1 and d_(2,1)=1+q\u00b2, so M_A^vee=A w_0\u2295A(1+q\u00b2)^\u22121w_1\u2295A w_2, properly containing M_A. Its middle quotient is isomorphic to A/(1+q\u00b2). Both lattices extend to the same A0 crystal lattice and both basis residues are the same three crystal elements. The upper operators are the weighted adjoints from the duality theorem; their q0 reductions give the same finite string, while exact lower adjunction differs by the displayed norm ratios. The upper basis is fixed by the ADJOINT bar, generally not the ordinary lower bar.",
        "strategy": "Use the exact rank-one contravariant recurrence from the Cartan-projection/module-form supplier: E F^(n)v=[m\u2212n+1]F^(n\u22121)v and sigma(F)=q^-1 K E give d_(m,n)=q^(m\u22122n+1)[m\u2212n+1]/[n] times d_(m,n\u22121), with d_(m,0)=1. Iterating yields the stated Gaussian norm; distinct labels are orthogonal. Invert these field diagonal entries to obtain the genuine dual basis. For m=2 the middle norm is q[2]=1+q\u00b2; it is not a unit of A, so (1+q\u00b2)^\u22121w_1 is outside the lower lattice. The quotient of its coefficient module by A is isomorphic to A/(1+q\u00b2), exhibiting the actual pair of lattices rather than a misleading 'index of E'. At0 this norm is an A0-unit\u22611; both basis residues agree. The finite-string ratio formulas then give the exact upper/lower adjoint operators and their modulo-q agreement. Ordinary bar fixes w_1 but sends its upper scalar denominator to(1+q^-2), so only the adjoint bar fixes w_1^up, exactly as the duality theorem states. All calculations are finite and choice-free apart from inherited general supplier assumptions.",
